

Create in-scope list (example)
nano inscope.txt

Paste something like this (one pattern per line):

*.target.com
api.target.com
app.target.com
admin.target.com

Create out-of-scope list (optional but useful)
nano outscope.txt

blog.target.com
shop.target.com
status.target.com
*.thirdparty.com

3. Filter Alive Domains (Best Method)

Assuming your alive domains are in live_target.com.txt:

Method A: Simple & Effective (Recommended)

Keep only In-Scope domains
cat live_target.com.txt | grep -E '\.target\.com' | tee inscope_live.txt

Or more precise:

cat live_target.com.txt | grep -iE '(^|\.)target\.com' > inscope_live.txt

Method B: Using a proper scope checker (better for wildcards)

Install a helpful tool:

go install github.com/tomnomnom/hacks/filter-scope@latest

Then:

cat live_target.com.txt | filter-scope inscope.txt > inscope_live.txt

4. Clean Version (Remove http/https)

Many people prefer only the domain names:

cat live_target.com.txt | sed 's|https\?://||' | sed 's|/.*||' > clean_live.txt

Then filter:

cat clean_live.txt | grep -E '\.target\.com$' > inscope_domains.txt

5. Full Practical Command (One-liner)

cat live_$DOMAIN.txt | sed 's|https\?://||; s|/.*||' | grep -E '\.target\.com$' | tee inscope_live.txt

Bonus: Automatic Separation Script

You can create this small script:

nano filter_scope.sh

Paste:

#!/bin/bash

ALIVE=$1
INSCOPE="inscope.txt"

echo "[+] Filtering In-Scope domains..."
cat $ALIVE | sed 's|https\?://||; s|/.||' | grep -Ef <(sed 's/\./\\./g; s/\/.*/g' $INSCOPE) | tee inscope_live.txt

echo "[+] Done! In-scope live domains saved to inscope_live.txt"

Make it executable and run:

chmod +x filter_scope.sh
./filter_scope.sh live_target.com.txt

